{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"trackW STILL 45! Even after counterAxisSizingMode='FIXED' + resize(311). Something else is wrong.\n\nHmm. Let me think about the structure again:\n\nProgress Row (VERTICAL, w311 now FIXED)\n├─ Top (HORIZONTAL, layoutAlign STRETCH?) — dump said alignSTRETCH, w145\n└─ Bottom (HORIZONTAL, alignSTRETCH, w100)\n   ├─ Track (lmNONE!, grow1, w45)\n   │   └─ Fill rect w164\n   └─ Left text w47\n\nWait — the dump said Track \"lmNONE\" — Track is not auto-layout, has layoutGrow=1. In Bottom (horizontal AL): Track grow1 → fills remaining primary (horizontal) space: 311-47-8gap... wait Bottom gap? If Bottom itemSpacing 8: Track = 311-47-8 = 256.\n\nBut Bottom itself stays w100?! layoutAlign='STRETCH' on Bottom within vertical parent... \n\nOH WAIT. I see it now! In Figma plugin API, for a child of an auto-layout frame, `layoutAlign: 'STRETCH'` stretches the child to fill the parent's COUNTER axis. For Bottom inside a VERTICAL parent: counter axis = horizontal ✓ should stretch to 311.\n\nUnless... `layoutAlign` values: 'MIN' | 'CENTER' | 'MAX' | 'STRETCH' | 'INHERIT'. STRETCH is correct.\n\nHmm, but hold on — did my earlier fix call actually SET anything? In the previous call I set topF.layoutAlign='STRETCH' when it was already STRETCH. And in this call I only set counterAxisSizingMode + resize. trackW read AFTER resize... still 45.\n\nMaybe the problem: `pr.resize(311, pr.height)` on an auto-layout frame with counterAxisSizingMode FIXED — fine. Then Bottom STRETCH should become 311. But measured 45 for Track immediately after — maybe layout recompute is LAZY and .width returns stale cached value until relayout happens (e.g., on next access of parent or after the call ends). The earlier screenshot (taken after previous calls) DID show overlap with Fill overflowing — consistent with Bottom=100, Track=45.\n\nActually wait — is it possible that `layoutAlign='STRETCH'` is being ignored because the child also has `layoutGrow` set? No...\n\nAlternatively, maybe Bottom has `counterAxisSizingMode='AUTO'` AND `primaryAxisSizingMode='FIXED'` w100 explicitly set — and STRETCH overrides width... it should.\n\nLet me try a different approach that avoids layoutAlign entirely: directly set Bottom's width via resize + primaryAxisSizingMode='FIXED'? But in an auto-layout parent, child sizing along the COUNTER axis (width, for vertical parent) is controlled by child's counterAxisSizingMode: FIXED vs AUTO. Setting bottom.counterAxisSizingMode='FIXED' and bottom.resize(311, 14) would pin width to 311. That definitely works (like the segments in Segmented Control where I used layoutAlign STRETCH + layoutGrow 1 successfully — hmm, those DID work... because the Segmented Control parent had FIXED counter width 343).\n\nKEY DIFFERENCE: Segmented Control (8:29): created with resize(343,36) and counterAxisSizingMode... I set 'FIXED' in stack() default (o.hugC undefined → FIXED), then resize(343,36). Children segments STRETCH worked ✓.\n\nProgress Row: created via stack('VERTICAL',4,{hugP?...}) — from Call 6: \"Progress Row 10:11 (311×35): vertical gap 4\" — I don't remember exact flags. The dump shows component w311 h35. If its counterAxisSizingMode was 'AUTO' (hug width), then children width = max child width... Top w145, Bottom w100 → hug would be 145, not 311! But component IS 311. So hug would compute 145... unless resize() forced 311 display while sizingMode stayed AUTO and children didn't restretch.\n\nThat's exactly the classic gotcha: setting `.resize()` on an auto-layout frame overrides size but sizingMode props remain AUTO; children with STRETCH inside an AUTO-counter parent behave as hug → no stretch.\n\nI set counterAxisSizingMode='FIXED' in THIS call though! And still trackW 45. Hmm, unless setting counterAxisSizingMode then resize... the resize might have reset it? Order: I set FIXED then resize. resize doesn't change sizing modes.\n\nBut wait — is the width axis the COUNTER axis here? layoutMode='VERTICAL' → primary axis = Y (vertical), counter = X (horizontal). counterAxisSizingMode='FIXED' → width fixed ✓.\n\nThen why is Track still 45?? Unless the width change didn't propagate because Bottom's layoutAlign is 'STRETCH'... which in a FIXED-counter parent should stretch. UNLESS Bottom's layoutAlign was somehow reset to 'INHERIT' — the dump showed alignSTRETCH before, and I never changed it since.\n\nHmm hold on, maybe the issue is the LAZY layout: width values (dtrk.width) are computed lazily and return stale values within the same execution. The Segmented Control children worked at creation because... they were created fresh. For EXISTING nodes, property reads may be stale until the scene graph flushes.\n\nTest: in this next call, just READ pr/Bottom/Track widths fresh (new execution → layout flushed). If they're correct now (256 etc.), the previous call actually worked and only the stale read misled me — and setRow used stale tk.width=45 → Fill widths are WRONG (small): setRow used tk.width>10?tk.width:251 → tk.width=45 stale → Fill = frac*45 ≈ 29px — too small. Need to re-run setRow after confirming real widths.\n\nSo next call: read widths first; then re-run setRow using the fresh values. If fresh Track width is 256, setRow will size correctly. Let me do: read + setRow in same call but read BEFORE setRow (fresh execution gives flushed layout from previous call's changes).\n\nAlso verify pr width/Top width.","signature":"7ae2e2164bce4514a626d0af"}],"id":"e7ba188c-3b57-400e-b5f2-47b730ce7469","model":"glm-5.3-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88fa287b-e3f1-4759-ae42-7b214809a163","uuid":"ee8f211a-0f40-4c5e-a6d4-971db9ef711f","timestamp":"2026-11-02T09:05:10.147Z","thinking_duration_ms":44316}
